    "abstract": "This report summarises the findings of the Marine Spatial Planning Addressing Climate Effects project (MSPACE, 2021-2026) in Northern Ireland's marine waters (Northern Ireland, UK). It includes key findings from novel research into: 1) the potential effects of climate change in this region of UK marine waters; 2) the structure of the region's blue economy; and 3) knowledge co-creation activities with marine stakeholders in the region. Through these activities, we draw up key recommendations for climate-resilient, economically sensible and socially acceptable strategies to manage marine space in Northern Ireland, leading to climate change adaptation and mitigation.",
    "field": "Marine spatial planning for climate change",
    "problem": "The paper addresses how siloed approaches to policy and governance in marine spatial planning limit opportunities to deliver climate action, creating a need for integrated, evidence-based scenarios that are economically viable and socially acceptable to support climate change adaptation and mitigation in Northern Ireland.",
    "method": "This paper proposes a method using co-created, climate-smart spatial management scenarios, which are evaluated through social acceptability surveys and multi-criteria decision analysis, to develop economically viable and socially acceptable marine plans that address climate change adaptation and mitigation in Northern Ireland.",
    "task": "The task is to explore alternative spatial management scenarios for Northern Ireland's marine waters to provide recommendations for its marine plan that support climate change adaptation and mitigation.",
